1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,852
A model migration can compile and still break behavior.

2
00:00:03,852 --> 00:00:10,699
Streaming, structured output, tool arguments, and retries are contracts that a plausible patch can silently change.

3
00:00:10,699 --> 00:00:13,267
PortVerdict puts those migrations on trial.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,477
This is an authenticated, recorded experiment, not a mock sponsor integration.

5
00:00:19,477 --> 00:00:22,733
Anyone can inspect the evidence without an account.

6
00:00:22,733 --> 00:00:28,431
New execution stays in an owner-only runner, so public visitors cannot spend API credits.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,336
The runner discovers the exact NVIDIA model from the authenticated Nebius catalog.

8
00:00:35,336 --> 00:00:38,003
This evaluation uses Nemotron Three Super.

9
00:00:38,003 --> 00:00:44,228
It generates three different migration strategies: minimal compatibility, prompt and schema adaptation, and resilience.

10
00:00:44,228 --> 00:00:50,008
Each proposal has provider response identities, measured token usage, and a source hash.

11
00:00:50,008 --> 00:00:54,010
Model commentary never decides whether the code is safe.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:58,529
Nebius Token Factory Sandboxes provide the controlled experiment.

13
00:00:58,529 --> 00:01:02,939
All three candidate operations start from the same immutable checkpoint.

14
00:01:02,939 --> 00:01:07,791
The proposed code runs as a non-root user with networking disabled.

15
00:01:07,791 --> 00:01:13,525
Fixed tests, source hashes, operation identities, and resource observations make each branch attributable.

16
00:01:13,525 --> 00:01:17,936
A separate single-shot proposal is tested from that same checkpoint.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,575
Here is a losing branch from the tool calling case.

18
00:01:23,575 --> 00:01:26,078
Its build passed, but behavioral contracts failed.

19
00:01:26,078 --> 00:01:30,010
The real logs preserve the failing checks, exit codes, and timings.

20
00:01:30,010 --> 00:01:32,513
Any failed hard gate rejects the candidate.

21
00:01:32,513 --> 00:01:36,803
This view keeps the proposed diff even when that branch cannot ship.

22
00:01:36,803 --> 00:01:39,663
A hash verified artifact backs the readable evidence.

23
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,102
Tavily performs real Search and Extract calls for official compatibility guidance.

24
00:01:50,102 --> 00:01:56,132
The recorded evidence preserves request identities, source URLs, retrieval times, and content hashes.

25
00:01:56,132 --> 00:02:04,481
Bounded excerpts inform generation, but retrieved text stays untrusted and cannot change execution policy or the fixed tests.

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,133
In this recorded case, PortVerdict selects a candidate only after every required hard gate passes.

27
00:02:11,133 --> 00:02:15,222
3 of the three candidate proposals satisfied all required gates.

28
00:02:15,222 --> 00:02:20,129
The three-case suite covers structured output, tool calling, and streaming with retries.

29
00:02:20,129 --> 00:02:24,218
Results are descriptive, not a statistical claim of model superiority.

30
00:02:24,218 --> 00:02:28,716
Inspect the adverse outcomes and the single-shot baselines alongside the tournament.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,919
The public repository includes the Apache licensed source, immutable evidence, reproducible tests, and the owner-run workflow.

32
00:02:36,919 --> 00:02:40,378
Earlier failed development attempts are documented, not hidden.

33
00:02:40,378 --> 00:02:47,729
PortVerdict turns a model migration into an experiment you can inspect, and a verdict you can defend.

